    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "can u explain why ceramic brake pads sometimes experience sudden thermal fade during aggressive high-speed deceleration compared to semi-metallic ones? specifically looking at the heat soak effect in the calipers.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ceramic brake pads are often chosen for their smooth, quiet performance and their resistance to fading at lower temperatures, but they can sometimes experience sudden thermal fade during aggressive high-speed deceleration. This is due to their lower thermal conductivity compared to semi-metallic pads. When you push the pedal hard, the calipers heat up and the brake pads absorb this heat. Ceramic pads, being less conductive, can't dissipate the heat as quickly, leading to a sudden fade. Semi-metallic pads, on the other hand, have better heat dissipation due to their metallic content, which helps them maintain their performance even under intense conditions. It's like comparing a ceramic pot to a metal pan; the metal pan heats up and cools down faster, making it better for cooking on a hot stove. The ceramic pot, while still functional, takes longer to adjust to temperature changes, leading to uneven cooking. Similarly, ceramic brake pads take longer to adjust to the heat generated during aggressive braking, leading to sudden thermal fade.",
      "source": "target",
